.git
.gitignore
.env
.venv
__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
docs/
presentation/
eval/
*.md
!requirements.txt
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml

# Research, not product. `experiments/` holds the M1/M2 packages plus their cached embeddings and
# per-experiment outputs; the running service reads none of it — everything it needs was promoted
# into data/artifacts/ by scripts/promote_artifacts.py. Excluding it keeps the build context small
# and makes the ingest/query separation physical rather than merely intended.
experiments/
scripts/
# ...except the one script the image genuinely runs. The build pulls the index and thumbnails from
# the dataset repo, and re-implementing that as an inline `python -c` would be a second copy of
# logic that already has to exist for local development.
!scripts/fetch_assets.py
tests/
pytest.ini

# Frontend build inputs and outputs. The image gets `FE/dist` from the node stage, never from the
# host: node_modules is ~200 MB of build context that also lands in the runtime image via
# `COPY . .`, and a locally-built dist/ would be a stale artifact silently overriding the one this
# build produced. Sources are still copied — the node stage needs them.
FE/node_modules/
FE/dist/

# The index and the thumbnails come from the dataset repo in a dedicated build step, never from the
# host. A developer who has run scripts/fetch_assets.py has 117 MB sitting in these paths, and
# `COPY . .` would bake that local copy into the image — silently overriding the pull with whatever
# happens to be on one machine, which is the same stale-artifact trap FE/dist is excluded for above.
# The four small artifacts beside them are committed and DO belong in the image, so this excludes
# the two matrices by name rather than the whole directory.
# The ingest working directories. Neither is in git, so a CI checkout has neither and this changes
# nothing there — but a developer's machine has both, and `docker build` sends the context before it
# reads a single instruction. Measured 2026-08-20: the tracked context is 14 MB, while these two add
# **1.09 GB** (staging 177 MB, extracted keyframes 909 MB) to every local build of an image that
# never reads them. `data/artifacts/*.jsonl` deliberately stays: the image needs it.
data/staging/
data/extracted_keyframes/
data/thumbs/
data/thumbs.tar
data/artifacts/vectors.npy
data/artifacts/caption_vectors.npy
# huggingface_hub's resume/etag bookkeeping, written beside whatever it downloads. Harmless but it
# is one machine's download state, and it has no business in a published image.
data/artifacts/.cache/
data/.cache/

# Local tooling and logs that must never reach a published image.
.ai-log/
.agents/
.claude/
.github/
data/chroma/
*.log
